+*   **Test-Time Evaluation:** Evaluating the model with the learned embeddings $e_i$ active will result in images that match the specific lighting of the source image $i$, which may appear inconsistent across views if the goal is a canonical representation. Setting $e_i = \mathbf{0}$ is required for the "photometric average" behavior [S1].
+*   **Photometric Variation:** Standard metrics like PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS are difficult to interpret on datasets with significant photometric variation because they penalize global brightness/color shifts. The sources note that the "w/GLO" model performs poorly on these raw metrics compared to baselines that attempt to "explain away" variation, but produces visually consistent results [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Affine Generative Latent Optimization
-A variant that allocates a 128-length vector for each image and uses these vectors as input to a two-layer MLP with 128 hidden units [S2]. The MLP outputs two vectors (scale and shift) of the same length as the bottleneck vector [S2]. The internal activation is ReLU, and the final activation for the scaling output is exp [S2]. The scale and shift are applied to the bottleneck vector before evaluating the view-dependent MLP [S2]. This technique is only used for video results and does not improve quantitative performance on metrics [S2].
+### [Affine Generative Latent Optimization](3497-affine-generative-latent-optimization.md)
+Affine Generative Latent Optimization (Affine GLO) is a technique for modulating the internal representations of a neural radiance field to capture per-image appearance variations, particularly for video sequences. It solves the problem of static NeRF models failing to represent dynamic lighting or texture changes across frames by applying an affine transformation to the bottleneck vector before view-dependent color prediction. It sets itself apart from standard [NeRF with Per-Image Embeddings](2484-nerf-with-per-image-embeddings.md) by learning a parametric mapping (an MLP) from a latent code to the affine parameters, rather than optimizing the embedding vectors directly, which allows for faster training and smoother interpolation.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** On the Tanks and Temples dataset (color-corrected metrics), the model achieves the following PSNR scores: bicycle (24.37), flowers (21.73), Outdoor garden (26.98), stump (26.40), treehill (22.87), room (31.63), counter (29.55), Indoor kitchen (32.23), bonsai (33.46) [S1]. The variant with GLO (w/GLO) achieves: bicycle (23.95), flowers (21.60), Outdoor garden (25.09), stump (25.98), treehill (21.99), room (28.24), counter (28.40), Indoor kitchen (30.81), bonsai (30.27) [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Neutralizes photometric variation during training, yielding consistent global brightness and color across views [S1]. Produces pleasing reconstructions that correspond to the photometric average of all input cameras when the embedding is set to zero at test time [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Performs poorly on standard metrics (PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS) on datasets with significant photometric variation because it does not preserve the variation that these metrics might otherwise capture [S1]. The affine modulation variant does not improve quantitative performance [S2].
-*   **When to use:** When consistent global appearance across views is desired, and photometric variation in the input data is considered noise or undesirable [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When standard metrics (PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS) are the primary evaluation criterion on datasets with significant photometric variation, as the model's neutralization of variation will yield artificially low scores compared to baselines that preserve it [S1].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+*   **Memory:** Adds $O(N \cdot d)$ memory for the per-image embeddings, where $N$ is the number of training images and $d$ is the embedding dimension. This is negligible compared to the MLP weights for typical $N$ and $d$ [G].
+*   **Time:** Adds minimal overhead per training step for embedding lookup and concatenation. The optimization involves updating $N$ additional vectors, which scales linearly with $N$ but is generally fast [G].
+
